{"kind":"report","text":"Planner read of the journey views design at fce231e0ed2923e7474255aa368dc0fb974d5d7b: one finding (P1) and one recommendation. This does not yet meet the planner read that a9a3585c requires.\n\nCoverage: I read the whole design note (476 lines) first, then checked it against plan 032 (section 2b, J2 and J5, and section 4) and the MVP plan (people-only approval and controller review, lines 110-118). I listed all 44 light state pages. I read the text of needs-you, needs-partial and needs-reviewed. I viewed these renders: needs-you light 1536, needs-you dark 390, change-review light 1536 and work-active light 1536. I did not view the other states or the concepts; the checker's coverage in ae5ea4f6 stands for those.\n\nWhat fits: the five families and six flows match the request and plan 032. The three reasons on each Needs you item, the separation of reading, Mark reviewed and settling, the markers and controller labels for people, agents and checkers, the pinned commits, the original-request recovery and the empty-check wording all match the plan. Controller review on Change agrees with the MVP plan (ownerMayReview stated visibly). The visual quality is good on desktop and phone.\n\nP1. Catch-up shows a count but not the changes. Needs you says \"7 new entries since your last review\" and offers Mark reviewed, but no state lists those seven entries. needs-partial says \"6 new entries loaded\" and also shows none of them. A person who returns cannot see what changed. Mark reviewed then records review points for entries the person never saw, which contradicts the note's own rule that \"only a complete inspected range can advance its review point\". Plan 032 J5 requires that \"after any absence, the view shows everything that changed since mark reviewed\", and that a person returning on another device \"sees every item that arrived\". The obligations list is one part of catch-up. The changes since the review point are the other part.\n\nFix, within the design-only scope: add a \"Since your last review\" list to Needs you, reusing the Work view's Recent record pattern. Group it by scope. Show one line per entry: entry number, actor marker and kind (with controller), what happened in plain words, and a link to the subject at that exact entry. Bound it, offer Load more, and name what was not loaded. Mark reviewed must cover exactly the ranges listed, in the same way that needs-partial already keeps the unread scope's point. Show the list in the needs-you, needs-partial and needs-reviewed states (in needs-reviewed it is empty: \"Nothing new since your review\"), in both themes and at both widths. On a phone the list may follow the obligations or sit behind a disclosure, as long as Mark reviewed cannot be reached without the list being reachable first. Add the seven fixture entries the note already counts (5, 1 and 1 across the three scopes) as named entries. Extend the walkthrough checks so that Mark reviewed advances only the ranges shown.\n\nR1 (recommendation, not blocking). The checker's outlined square looks like an unticked checkbox, for example beside \"Passed for integration 6ef5643\" on change-review. People may read it as \"not done\", or try to click it. Consider a checker marker that does not look like a form control, such as an outlined diamond or a square with an inner rule. The word \"checker\" and the result text stay as they are.\n\nWhen P1 is fixed and the checker approves the exact head, I will read the successor's Needs you states and their walkthrough again. That read is bounded to P1 and R1.","body":{"findings":"P1 catch-up lists no changes since the review point; R1 checker marker reads as a checkbox","head":"fce231e0ed2923e7474255aa368dc0fb974d5d7b","planner_read":"not yet met","request":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:d035139b33fd62ce0b55c50b33cc7bdf9f8ad306","to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}